  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 337 AM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nTODAY AND TONIGHT...\n\nLight rain lingered over northwest Wisconsin east of a line \nfrom Spooner to Mercer. Another band of showers was advancing \neast-southeast along the North Dakota/Manitoba border east into \nnorthwest Ontario. Between these two areas of precipitation a \nwedge of clear skies was found. Areas of fog are anticipated \nthis morning as winds become light. The lowest visibility will \nbe found over areas that received rainfall Wednesday afternoon \nand evening. We will monitor trends. A Dense Fog Advisory may \nbecome necessary.\n\nThe light rain over northwest Wisconsin will push out of our \narea by 7 AM while the northern band of showers sags southward \ninto the Borderlands and gradually dissipates by 10 AM. \nOtherwise expect mostly sunny to partly cloudy skies today. \nHighs will reach the middle 60s along the Canadian border to the\nmiddle 70s in the St. Croix River Valley. Clouds spread \nnorthward tonight ahead of an approaching low pressure system. \nRain chances will likely hold off until after 7 AM Friday due to\nthe influence of high pressure moving eastward across southern \nManitoba into northwest Ontario. More on that below.\n\nFRIDAY AND SATURDAY...\n\nSurface low pressure and associated shortwave trough will \npropagate eastward from the South Dakota/Nebraska border to near\nsouthern Lake Michigan by Saturday evening. A warm front will \nlift northward across Iowa to near the Minnesota border and \nstretch east into southern Wisconsin by Friday evening. \nIsentropic ascent over the warm frontal zone will produce a \nbroad area of precipitation over southern and central Minnesota \nstretching into northwest Wisconsin on Friday. The influence of \nthe northern surface high pressure will be most noticeable \nthrough reinforcing dry air from north to south undercutting the\nprecipitation. Radar echoes are likely to over-represent the \nnorthward extent of precipitation as the dry air evaporates the \nrain before it can reach the ground. We continued the previous \nshift's concept of tightening the QPF and PoP gradient over \ncentral Minnesota into northern Wisconsin.\n\nNBM probabilities support the tight gradient of precip and \nPoPs. The NBM probability of more than 1 inch of rain is \nmaximized south of our forecast area. There is a stripe of \n40-60% chance of 1 inch or more south of a line from Brainerd to\nSandstone to Hayward to Park Falls. The 40% threshold for 0.5 \nis not much farther north and extends from roughly Pine River to\nMoose Lake to Mellen to Mercer. The 40% chance of a tenth of an\ninch extends from Walker to Two Harbors to Bayfield to \nOntonagon, MI. The distance of the 40% chance of 1 inch of rain \nand a tenth of an inch is only about 60 miles.\n\nRain tapers off Saturday morning with a small portion of \nnorthwest Wisconsin seeing a 30% chance of showers into the \nearly afternoon.\n\nAs the high and low pressure systems propagate eastward winds \nover western Lake Superior will turn northeasterly and \nstrengthen Friday and remain strong Saturday. The highest wind \nand waves will be in the southwest arm of Lake Superior which \nwill pose a risk of rip currents for the beaches of the Twin \nPorts. \n\nSATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH NEXT THURSDAY...\n\nAnother area of surface high pressure will propagate eastward \nacross the Canadian Prairies and northern Ontario over the \nweekend. Rain chances Sunday have dwindled as the next area of \nlow pressure to our south has taken a more southerly trajectory \nthan earlier forecasts. A few sprinkles Sunday afternoon and \nevening aren't out of the question.\n\nCooler air, clear skies, and light winds will set the stage for\nareas of fog and frost Sunday night and again Monday night. \nLows both nights are forecast in the low 40s to middle 30s.\n\nTemperatures Sunday through Thursday will be near normal. They \nwill start out on the cool side of the normal range Sunday with \nhighs in the low 60s to around 70 degrees. Temps gradually warm \nto the warm side of the normal range by Wednesday and Thursday \nwith highs in the middle 60s to low and middle 70s.\n\nA chance of rain returns Wednesday night and Thursday, although\noverall confidence is low at this time.",
